--TEST--
`rule:types/decimal`: `Core\Decimal::allocate` refuses an empty, an all-zero and a negative ratio list as bad arguments, and a share no `decimal` holds as arithmetic
--FILE--
<?nvs
decimal $one = 1.00;

// Three lists that name no split at all: no parts to make, parts that every
// ratio asks to be empty, and a part that would owe. None of them is an
// arithmetic that overflowed, so each is a `LogicError` -- the class for an
// argument the call cannot mean anything with.
array<decimal> $none = [];
try {
    echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Decimal::allocate($one, $none)), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $empty) {
    echo $empty->message, "\n";
}
try {
    echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Decimal::allocate($one, [0, 0])), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $nothing) {
    echo $nothing->message, "\n";
}
decimal $unit = 1;
decimal $owes = 0 - $unit;
try {
    echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Decimal::allocate($one, [2, $owes])), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $owing) {
    echo $owing->message, "\n";
}

// The bound the split shares with the operators, reached from the side it has
// of its own: an amount already at 28 fractional digits and a ratio with any
// scale at all want one digit more than the type has. `ArithmeticError` here,
// because this one is the overflow `rule:types/arithmetic` names.
decimal $smallest = 0.0000000000000000000000000001;
try {
    echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Decimal::allocate($smallest, [0.5, 0.5])), "\n";
} catch (ArithmeticError $wide) {
    echo $wide->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Decimal::allocate needs at least one ratio to split 1.00 by
Core\Decimal::allocate needs a ratio above zero to split 1.00 by
Core\Decimal::allocate has no share for the negative ratio -1
Core\Decimal::allocate cannot split 0.0000000000000000000000000001 at scale 28 by these ratios: a `decimal` holds 28 fractional digits and a 96-bit mantissa
